# Intermediate Activities Guide

To do these activities, you need an HTML file to hold your buttons and inputs.

## 1. Setup HTML (`index.html`)

Create a new file named `index.html` in the same folder and add this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>JS Practice</title>
</head>
<body>
    
    <!-- Area for Intermediate Activities -->
    <h2>Sum Calculator</h2>
    <input type="number" id="num1" placeholder="Number 1">
    <input type="number" id="num2" placeholder="Number 2">
    <button id="sumBtn">Calculate Sum</button>
    <p>Result: <span id="resultDisplay">0</span></p>

    <script src="intermediate.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

## 2. The JavaScript Code (`intermediate.js`)

Create a file named `intermediate.js` and add this code:

```javascript
// 1. Create a function that accepts two numbers
// 2. Return their sum
function addNumbers(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

// 3. Use a button click to display the result in the HTML panel
let button = document.getElementById("sumBtn");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    // Get values from the inputs
    let n1 = Number(document.getElementById("num1").value);
    let n2 = Number(document.getElementById("num2").value);

    // Call our function
    let sum = addNumbers(n1, n2);

    // Display result
    document.getElementById("resultDisplay").innerText = sum;
});
```

## Explanation & "How To"

### Functions
**Goal:** Reuse code. You give it inputs (arguments), it does work, and gives back an output (return).
*   `function name(inputs) { ... }` defines it.
*   `return` sends data back.

### Event Listeners (`addEventListener`)
**Goal:** Wait for the user to do something (like click).

**Pros & Cons vs `onclick=""` in HTML:**
*   **`addEventListener` (The Modern Way)**
    *   *Pros:* detailed control. You can add multiple listeners to the same button. Keeps HTML clean (Separation of Concerns).
    *   *Cons:* Slightly more code to write.
*   **`onclick="..."` (The Old/Simple Way)**
    *   *Pros:* Very fast to write for simple tests.
    *   *Cons:* Clutters HTML. overwrites any previous click handlers. **Avoid** for large apps.

### `document.getElementById`
**Goal:** Grab an element from your HTML so JavaScript can change it or read it.
*   `id="..."` in HTML must match exactly.
